    <div class="article-content">
      <p style="font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 28px;">
        There is a moment on the morning ferry from Tarifa, Spain when Europe and Africa exist in the same breath. Stand on the aft deck with a warm cortado in hand: behind you, the whitewashed ramparts of Andalusia recede into the morning haze; ahead, just nine nautical miles across the wind-whipped waters of the Strait of Gibraltar, the green hills of Morocco rise out of the Atlantic.
      </p>

      <p>
        For eight centuries, Southern Spain and Morocco were not two separate worlds divided by a geopolitical boundary—they were a single, luminous civilization known as <strong>Al-Andalus</strong>. The intricate cedar wood carvings of the Alhambra in Granada speak the exact same geometric mathematical language as the Medersa Bou Inania in Fes. The olive oil traditions of Andalusia mirror the groves of Meknes. Even the blue doors of Chefchaouen were painted by Jewish and Muslim families fleeing the Reconquista, carrying the heavy iron keys to their lost Andalusian courtyards in their coat pockets.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you have two weeks for vacation, combining <strong>Spain and Morocco in 14 days</strong> is the most culturally rewarding journey on the Mediterranean. In this guide, we lay out the exact, stress-free route: how to link Spain's high-speed rail network with a seamless private chauffeur in Morocco, avoiding luggage friction, border confusion, and tourist burnout.
      </p>

      <div class="roam-field-note" style="margin: 32px 0; background: #faf8f5; border-left: 4px solid #C8A45C; padding: 22px 26px;">
        <span class="roam-field-note-kicker" style="font-size: 11px; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .1em; color: #C8A45C; text-transform: uppercase;">Curator's Philosophy // Hamza &amp; Abdelhakim</span>
        <p style="margin: 8px 0 0; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; color: #333;">
          &ldquo;Most travelers make the mistake of treating Spain and Morocco as two disconnected vacations stitched together with a budget flight. But when you cross the Strait by sea and step directly into Tangier with your own local Berber driver waiting at the terminal gates, you feel the living continuity of history. You are not just sightseeing; you are following an ancient caravan of poets, architects, and scholars.&rdquo;
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>14-Day Route Overview: Pacing &amp; Logistics</h2>
